16.27.m:
With this amulet Indra slew Vṛtra;
with this, the wise one routed the Asuras;
with this he conquered both heaven and earth;
with this he conquered the four quarters.

16.28.m:
Let Indra deposit manly power in him;
enter into him together, O gods,
for long life of a hundred autumns,
so that he may be full of life and attain old age.

16.30.m:
Messengers of Death, lead yonder foes away;
messengers of Yama, bind them down!
Let more than thousands be slain;
let […] crush them!

The Sādhyas go raising up one net-pole
by their might;
the Rudras the second, the Vasus the third;
by the Ādityas one is raised.

16.32.m:
The gods generated the whip of honey;
her embryo became all-formed.
The mother nourishes him, born young;
he, when born, covers all worlds.

To her, the swelling one, the waters draw near;
to her, the mighty bulls, to her, the self-rulers.
They rain, they make it rain;
the waters know that desire and vigor.

16.33.m:
As a bull you cast potency on the earth from heaven;
with the whip of honey you anoint the earth.
All cattle subsist upon her when born;
by it, she fills them with vigor.

16.35.m:
Let sorceries be for the sorcery-maker,
curses for the curser;
we send her back in reverse;
may she reach him who made her.

You […] the practitioners of sorcery, the cunning ones;
we know this spoiler of sorcery,
reversing the path, returning again;
with it we wash you clean.

16.36.m:
Brought near, discovered, buried,
creeping as hostility, we have found the makers;
let it go to where it came from; there, like a horse,
let it roll; let it strike the offspring of the sorcery-maker.

16.37.m:
Go away, O sorcery, do not stay;
follow the track as of one pierced [by an arrow].
He is the hunter;
he has no right to injure you.

16.38.m:
Wherever you have been placed,
from there we raise you up;
become lighter than a leaf!

16.41.m:
Turning toward you, who face toward me,
O dwelling, I enter you who do no harm;
for within are fire and waters,
both the first-born of truth.

16.42.m:
Sūrya fastened it on;
with it he conquered these quarters;
it milks luster for him.
[…]

16.44.m:
Dhātṛ fastened it on;
he formed the beings.
With that do you slay the haters!

16.46.m:
Earth you are, patient you are;
your two mouths are: "What has been" and "What has well been";
unto those two let me worship you; unto those two be homage to you;
with those two be gracious to us, with those two speak for us, with those two smell us!
Like a mother to a son, like a father to a boy,
this one of yours I am; release me from Grāhi,
from bonds! Lead this Vimada [forth]!

16.48.m:
All you are, and all this is yours;
your two mouths are: "Universe" and "All-gods";
unto those two let me worship you; unto those two be homage to you;
with those two be gracious to us, with those two speak for us, with those two smell us!
Like a mother to a son, like a father to a boy,
this one of yours I am; release me from Grāhi,
from bonds! Lead this Vimada [forth]!
